---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy - Arthur Pfalzgraf" description="Privacy notice for arthurpfz.com and croissantride.com">
  <article class="legal">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="meta">Applies to arthurpfz.com and croissantride.com · Last updated 2026-11-10</p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      Arthur Pfalzgraf, Germany. Questions about your data: <a href="mailto:ar.pfalzgraf@gmail.com">ar.pfalzgraf@gmail.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Short version</h2>
    <p>
      No accounts, no cookies, no ads, no tracking. arthurpfz.com counts page views with cookieless analytics that I host
      myself; Croissant Ride has no analytics. The only personal data that reaches a server is what every website request
      carries (your IP address and browser details), handled by the host and the analytics tool below.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      Both sites are hosted by Netlify, Inc., 101 2nd Street, San Francisco, CA 94105, USA. To deliver the pages and keep
      them secure, Netlify processes the data each request carries: IP address, date and time, page requested, referring
      page, browser and operating system. I don't use these logs for statistics or to identify anyone. Netlify acts as my
      processor under its Data Processing Agreement. Data may be processed in the USA; Netlify is certified under the
      EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework (Art. 45 GDPR). Legal basis: Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, my legitimate interest in running
      the sites reliably and securely.
    </p>

    <h2>Analytics (arthurpfz.com only)</h2>
    <p>
      arthurpfz.com uses Umami, an open-source analytics tool that I host myself on a server in Frankfurt, Germany. It
      sets no cookies and stores nothing on your device. It records page views, the referring site, and the browser,
      operating system, device type and country, and it does not store your IP address. Visitors cannot be identified or
      followed across sites, and the data is not shared with anyone. Browsers that send "Do Not Track" are not counted.
      Legal basis: my legitimate interest in knowing how the site is used (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
    </p>

    <h2>Croissant Ride</h2>
    <p>
      Croissant Ride (croissantride.com) runs entirely in your browser. Your settings (FTP, weight, units, sound, chosen
      world), imported sessions and a crash-safe copy of the ride in progress are kept in your browser's local storage on
      your device. They never reach me or any server, and you can delete them by clearing the site's data in your browser.
      This storage is needed for the service you asked for (§ 25(2) no. 2 TDDDG), so no consent banner is shown.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bluetooth sensors (trainer, heart-rate strap, cadence sensor, remote) connect directly to your browser. Their
      readings, including heart rate, stay on your device until you download a ride file.
    </p>
    <p>
      A ride leaves your device only when you choose to: "Download .FIT" saves it to your computer, and the "Post to"
      buttons open the upload page of the service you pick (Intervals.icu, Strava, Garmin Connect, TrainingPeaks), where
      you upload the file yourself. What happens there is covered by that service's own privacy policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spoken cues use your browser's built-in voices. Some browsers (e.g. Chrome's Google voices, Edge's online voices)
      send the text of the cue, such as "240 watts, 10 minutes", to their maker's servers to speak it. Mute the cues to
      avoid this.
    </p>

    <h2>Fonts and other content</h2>
    <p>
      All fonts and scripts are served from the sites themselves, except the analytics script on arthurpfz.com, which
      comes from my own server (stats.arthurpfz.com, see Analytics). No content is loaded from anyone else's servers.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you can ask for access to your data (Art. 15), correction (Art. 16), deletion (Art. 17), restriction
      (Art. 18) and portability (Art. 20), and you can object to processing based on legitimate interest (Art. 21). Write
      to the email above. You can also complain to a data protection authority, e.g. the one in the EU country where you
      live.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .legal {
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-lg) 0;
  }

  .legal h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .legal p {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .legal a {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .meta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-meta);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
</style>
